    a: 'Session 1 for 2027 is tentatively on 22 to 24 and 28 to 30 January 2027, with 31 January as a buffer. NTA calls these dates tentative, so confirm on the NTA notice.',
  },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
  })),
};
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>The career options after 12th PCM are not a list of jobs. They are a ladder of nine rungs, and your JEE rank decides which rung you start on.</strong>
    JoSAA, the joint counselling for IITs, NITs, IIITs and other government-funded colleges, had 67,323 seats in 2026. About 15.4 lakh students sat JEE Main, roughly 23 for every seat. Yet those seats are only about 5.4% of all B.Tech admissions.
    <br /><br />
    The rung matters less than the skill portfolio you build on it. That portfolio unlocks higher-paying work and earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>71.9% of JoSAA seats (48,372) need only a JEE Main rank. The 18,951 IIT seats need Advanced, where 56,880 qualified, or 3.0 for each seat.</li>
      <li>Rank buys a branch or a college, rarely both. IIT Bombay computer science closed at 67 in 2026. IIT Bhilai closed at 8,886.</li>
      <li>About 95% of B.Tech students are outside JoSAA, so a missed rank is a change of route, not the end of engineering.</li>
      <li><strong>Check each college's NIRF sheet, then build one proof project. A skill portfolio you can show beats the rung name when you apply for your first job or a better one.</strong></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    Looking for every stream, not just PCM? Start with
    <a href="/blog/career-options/career-options-after-12th/">career options after 12th</a>.
    For the work-style map of PCM paths, read
    <a href="/blog/career-options/pcm-career-options/">PCM career options</a>.
    Below you get seats, ranks, outcomes and costs.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you are unsure which rung suits how you work, the
    <a href="/career-skills-compass/">Career &amp; Skills Compass</a> gives a quick read on your strengths.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="ladder">Career options after 12th PCM, rung by rung</h2>

  <p>
    Tap a filter to see only the rungs that fit your rank list.
  </p>

  <p>
    The order follows the tightest closing ranks first. It is not an order of pay. Pay depends on proof, as the NIRF numbers below show.
  </p>

  <div class="filter-bar" role="group" aria-label="Filter the rungs">
    {filters.map((f, i) => (
      <button type="button" class="filter-chip" data-filter={f.key} aria-pressed={i === 0 ? 'true' : 'false'}>{f.label}</button>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="filter-count" aria-live="polite" id="filter-count">Showing all 9 rungs.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table doors-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Rung</th>
          <th>Which rank list</th>
          <th>What it took</th>
          <th>Seats and crowd</th>
          <th>Money and outcome</th>
          <th>Watch out</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {rungs.map((d) => (
          <tr data-tags={d.tags}>
            <td data-label="Rung"><strong>{d.name}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Which rank list">{d.list}</td>
            <td data-label="What it took">{d.closing}</td>
            <td data-label="Seats and crowd">{d.seats}</td>
            <td data-label="Money and outcome">{d.money}</td>
            <td data-label="Watch out">{d.watch}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Sources: JEE Advanced 2026 result press release, NIRF 2025 engineering sheets, the AP higher-education fee order, NATA 2026 brochure, and news and exam-portal reports of JoSAA, BITSAT and AP counselling. Closing ranks are quoted from published reports of the JoSAA rounds, so check the official JoSAA rank search for your own category and quota. All closing ranks here are open gender-neutral. Reserved categories use category ranks.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      Read the table as a menu with prices, not a ranking of worth. A Rung 8 seat with a strong project can beat a Rung 5 seat with none.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="crowd">The seat arithmetic behind every rank</h2>

  <p>
    A closing rank means little without the crowd and the seat count. Here is the working, using only figures we could source.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>What we measured</th>
          <th>Numbers</th>
          <th>Working</th>
          <th>Result</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {crowdRows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="What we measured"><strong>{r.what}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Numbers">{r.numbers}</td>
            <td data-label="Working">{r.math}</td>
            <td data-label="Result">{r.result}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">Read these with care</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>15,38,468 includes students who never plan to use JoSAA, so 22.9 per seat is an upper bound on the real queue.</li>
      <li>The B.Tech admission count is from 2024-25 and the JoSAA seat count is from 2026. The years differ, so 5.4% is a scale check, not an exact share.</li>
      <li>The JoSAA seat counts come from a report of the seat matrix. Reports differ slightly on the IIT count, so treat 18,951 as "about 18,900".</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      Appeared and qualified counts for JEE Advanced come from the official 2026 result press release. JEE Main counts are NTA data as reported by a coaching site.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="branch">Branch or college? The same rank buys one, not both</h2>

  <p>
    Every year students ask whether to take a top college with a weak branch or a strong branch in a thinner college. The ranks show why it is hard.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    {driftCards.map((c) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">{c.label}</div>
        <h3>{c.title}</h3>
        <p>{c.body}</p>
        <p class="feature-card__watch"><span>Watch out:</span> {c.watch}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      If you must pick, choose the combination where you will do the work for four years. The closing rank shows how many students want a seat. It does not show how much the work will pay you.
    </p>
    <p>
      Computer science at a newer campus can be a fine start for a student who builds projects. It can be a poor one for a student who waits for a campus drive.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="outcomes">What "placed" hides: NIRF numbers for nine colleges</h2>

  <p>
    NIRF is the government's college-ranking framework. Its sheets are filled by the colleges themselves, but they show three things a brochure often hides. These are the share placed, the share in higher studies, and the rest.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>College</th>
          <th>Graduated</th>
          <th>Placed</th>
          <th>Higher studies</th>
          <th>Neither</th>
          <th>Median of the placed</th>
          <th>At most, share of the batch at or above that median</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {outcomeRows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="College"><strong>{r.college}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Graduated">{r.grads}</td>
            <td data-label="Placed">{r.placed}</td>
            <td data-label="Higher studies">{r.hs}</td>
            <td data-label="Neither">{r.neither}</td>
            <td data-label="Median of the placed">{r.median}</td>
            <td data-label="At most, share of the batch at or above that median">{r.cap}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    The median covers only placed students. Half of the placed earned that figure or more, so at most half of the placed share of the batch did. We halve the placed share for the last column. NIRF 2025 sheets (graduating year 2023-24) for the first seven and the last college; NIT Andhra Pradesh is the 2025 batch from the NIRF 2026 report as quoted by Careers360, covering all undergraduates. "Neither" can include founders and exam aspirants.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">Two trends to notice</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>NIT Patna's median went from Rs 9.88 lakh in 2022-23 to Rs 7.0 lakh in 2023-24, down 29%.</li>
      <li>NIT Andhra Pradesh went from Rs 7.56 lakh in the NIRF 2025 sheet to Rs 6.41 lakh in the NIRF 2026 report, down 15%. These two releases are not strictly like for like.</li>
      <li>A government-funded name did not protect either median.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      The honest read: a seat gives you a start. The next four years of proof decide where you finish.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="cost">Fees, first pay and the 10% rule</h2>

  <p>
    A degree should not eat the whole education budget. As a strict planning rule, treat about 10% of your family's total education budget as the starting limit for college fees. Treat 20% as a serious caution line.
  </p>

  <p>
    Here are four real fee levels from AP, set against one sampled entry salary. The salary is the PayScale India entry figure for a software engineer, Rs 5.37 lakh, from 4,950 profiles in July 2026. It is not a promise of a job.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Route</th>
          <th>Four-year fees</th>
          <th>Months of that entry pay</th>
          <th>Budget needed to stay at 10%</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {costRows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Route"><strong>{r.route}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Four-year fees">{r.fee}</td>
            <td data-label="Months of that entry pay">{r.months}</td>
            <td data-label="Budget needed to stay at 10%">{r.ten}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Fees are tuition and listed charges only. Hostel, travel, a laptop and exam fees are extra. The AP range comes from the state higher-education regulator's order for 2023-24 to 2025-26, read from a mirrored copy of the order. Check the order for 2026-27 before you budget. The private college figures come from one college page.
  </p>

  <p>
    These numbers say that the fee itself is rarely the big risk at low-fee colleges. The big risk is the "neither" share, because a fee paid with no job to follow is the real loss. A 39.2% "neither" rate at one college is a bigger warning than a Rs 1 lakh fee gap.
  </p>

  <p>
    The other 90% of your budget pays for living costs, a laptop, entrance fees, current online learning, projects and the upskilling you will need after the degree.
  </p>

  <p>
    The NPTEL exam costs Rs 1,000, and many free YouTube and open-course materials teach the same code. An engineering degree is still needed for most engineering jobs. It is not a full skill system.
  </p>

  <h2 id="claims">Popular PCM claims vs the record</h2>

  <p>
    Several pages that rank for this topic repeat the same weak claims. Here they are next to the figures.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>What you will read</th>
          <th>What the record shows</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {claimRows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="What you will read"><strong>{r.claim}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What the record shows">{r.rule}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      A number is only useful with its source and its year. Before you act on any pay claim, ask who counted it, how many people they counted and when.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="mix">Where the 12.5 lakh B.Tech students actually sit</h2>

  <p>
    AICTE counted about 12.53 lakh B.Tech admissions in 2024-25 against 14.9 lakh approved seats, a fill rate of about 84%. Five branches took almost nine in ten of them.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Branch group</th>
          <th>Admitted, 2024-25</th>
          <th>Share of 12.53 lakh</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {mixRows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Branch group"><strong>{r.branch}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Admitted, 2024-25">{r.n}</td>
            <td data-label="Share of 12.53 lakh">{r.share}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    From AICTE data as reported by a news site. The last row is the difference. The five named groups add to 10,86,442, which is 86.7% of the total. AICTE projected 15.98 lakh capacity for 2025-26.
  </p>

  <p>
    Computer science alone is almost one in three seats filled. A computer science degree puts you in the biggest crowd on the market.
  </p>

  <p>
    What separates you is the work you can show. That is why a salary range with no sample does not help you decide.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">Use The 4-Checkpoint Protocol before you pick a rung</h2>

  <p>
    Before you commit, check your Biology, Context, Market and Survival. This is The 4-Checkpoint Protocol.
  </p>

  <div class="protocol-stack">
    {checkpointItems.map((item) => (
      <div class="checkpoint">
        <div class="checkpoint__num">{item.num}</div>
        <div class="checkpoint__body">
          <div class="checkpoint__title">{item.title}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
          <div class="checkpoint__note">{item.note}</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="lower-rank">If the rank comes in lower than the plan</h2>

  <p>
    This is the moment that decides the year. There are two honest moves, and each has a cost you can count.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Repeat the exam</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Delays first pay by a year. An entry software salary is about Rs 5.37 lakh in the PayScale sample, so count that.</li>
        <li>Coaching adds a cost we could not source, so ask for the fee sheet.</li>
        <li>Makes sense when the gain moves you at least two rungs and the family can fund it.</li>
        <li>Needs a proof project running in the same year.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Join the best reachable seat</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Starts proof-building this year, not next year.</li>
        <li>Choose by NIRF sheet and fee, not by name.</li>
        <li>Keep later counselling rounds alive, since a round 3 close can be about 2.8 times looser than round 1.</li>
        <li>Keep BITSAT, state tests and non-engineering doors in the plan.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A drop year is a big bet when about 22.9 students compete for each JoSAA seat. A repeat attempt puts you in next year's crowd.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keep a second door open that costs little, such as a CUET form for a BSc or a state counselling slot. If engineering is no longer the plan, the guide on
      <a href="/blog/career-options/career-options-after-12th-except-engineering/">career options after 12th except engineering</a>
      has the dates, seats and age caps.
    </p>
    <p>
      A BSc in statistics, physics or data science, with a strong programming portfolio, can be a better bet than a B.Tech seat at a college with a high "neither" share. Check that college's NIRF sheet before you decide.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="filters">Five filters, not one</h2>

  <p>
    The ladder has more than one filter. A student can clear one and still face the next. This is the real shape of an engineering path.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    {filterCards.map((c) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">{c.label}</div>
        <h3>{c.title}</h3>
        <p>{c.body}</p>
        <p class="feature-card__fit"><span>{c.fit.split(':')[0]}:</span>{c.fit.slice(c.fit.indexOf(':') + 1)}</p>
        <p class="feature-card__watch"><span>{c.watch.split(':')[0]}:</span>{c.watch.slice(c.watch.indexOf(':') + 1)}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      The path with the most headroom is not a rung. It is ownership.
    </p>
    <p>
      A developer who ships products, freelances for clients or builds a small software business owns the price of the work and can hire help. A salary alone rarely reaches early financial freedom.
    </p>
    <p>
      That ownership path needs proof, sales sense and clear writing. No rank gives you those. Add one as you go.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="proof">The proof shelf and the multiplier skill for each rung</h2>

  <p>
    Employers and clients trust finished work more than a college name. Pick one proof shelf and build it deeply, instead of five half-built profiles.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Where you land</th>
          <th>Proof that counts</th>
          <th>Multiplier skill</th>
          <th>Where AI helps now, and what comes later</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {proofRows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Where you land"><strong>{r.group}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Proof that counts">{r.proof}</td>
            <td data-label="Multiplier skill">{r.skill}</td>
            <td data-label="Where AI helps now, and what comes later">{r.ai}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Start with strong free material. YouTube courses, NPTEL, open courseware and official documentation can teach most of the code you will use.
  </p>

  <p>
    A paid course needs to give you something more, such as expert feedback, accountability or a credential a target employer asks for. Judge each course on its syllabus, recent updates and learner outcomes, not its platform name.
  </p>

  <p>
    Explain your work clearly in English and in your home language. That one skill raises the value of every proof asset above. Practise it on one person outside engineering each month.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      One core skill, one multiplier skill and one visible proof asset is the start of a high-income skill portfolio. It is also what opens higher-paying options, whichever rung you start on.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="calendar">The calendar and your next 110 days</h2>

  <p>
    The 2026 cycle ran from January to a final JoSAA allotment on 16 July. The next one starts earlier than most students plan for.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Date</th>
          <th>Event</th>
          <th>What it means</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {calendarRows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Date"><strong>{r.date}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Event">{r.event}</td>
            <td data-label="What it means">{r.note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    From 4 October 2026 to 22 January 2027 is 110 days, the stretch before the tentative Session 1.
  </p>

  <p>
    In a Class 12 year, school and boards come first. Use weekday evenings for short practice blocks and one longer weekend block for a single project or paper.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you are in a drop year, add a fixed weekly proof task next to the mock tests. Treat the dates above as the shape of the year and re-check each on the official notice.
  </p>

  <div class="rich-list-wrap">
    <ol class="rich-list">
      <li><strong>Check your list.</strong> Put your Main and Advanced ranks, or your state rank, against the table. Find your rung and the one above and below it.</li>
      <li><strong>Pick two rungs and one backup.</strong> One that fits your work style, one that fits your family budget, and one that costs little to keep alive.</li>
      <li><strong>Run one small test on each.</strong> Code a small tool, finish one drawing set or solve one real problem. Give it as long as it honestly takes.</li>
      <li><strong>Write the decision down.</strong> One page: what you chose, what it costs, what you gave up and the date you will review it.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <h2 id="gates">Pass The 3 Gates before you commit a year</h2>

  <p>
    A good choice is one you can prove, explain and test. These are The 3 Gates: Proof of Skill, Proof of Communication and Proof of Value.
  </p>

  <div class="gates-block">
    <p class="gates-block__title">Pass these before you spend a year on any rung</p>
    <div class="gates-row">
      {gatesItems.map((g) => (
        <div class="gate">
          <span class="gate__num">{g.num}</span>
          <span class="gate__name">{g.name}</span>
          <p>{g.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="parents">Talking to your parents about rank, branch and a drop year</h2>

  <p>
    Parents worry about the unknown. Give them seats, fees and a review date.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Instead of</th>
          <th>Try</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {parentRows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Instead of">{r.say}</td>
            <td data-label="Try"><strong>{r.better}</strong></td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="mistakes">Expensive wrong turns on this ladder</h2>

  <div class="mistakes-list">
    {mistakes.map((item, i) => (
      <div class="mistake-item">
        <div class="mistake-item__num">{i + 1}</div>
        <div class="mistake-item__content">
          <div class="mistake-item__title">{item.title}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="next-step">What to do next</h2>

  <p>
    Tonight: find your rung in the table and write down the rung above and below it. This week: open the NIRF sheet for your top two colleges and read the "neither" column. This month: start one proof project and keep it public.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      The right rung plus visible proof is how a PCM student builds a high-value skill portfolio, reaches stronger income options and moves toward earlier financial freedom.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    If you want a second opinion on which rung fits your rank, money and work style, book
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">career guidance</a>.
    Or start with the free
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/assessments">career and skill assessments</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="keep-going">Keep going</h2>

  <div class="link-cards">
    <a class="link-card" href="/blog/career-options/pcm-career-options/">
